    {
        "content": "<p>I ask the following question out of ignorance (I am fairly new to Mathlib), and it's likely that the design choice discussed below has already been considered.</p>\n<p>Mathlib currently has the multivariate polynomial division algorithm packaged as <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RingTheory/MvPolynomial/Groebner.html#MonomialOrder.div\">MonomialOrder.div</a>.  The algorithm itself is described only within the proof, and not as a standalone function.</p>\n<p>(Note that the pair <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(g, r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> provided by the theorem is <em>not unique</em> in general. In particular, when several divisors can reduce the leading term, the division algorithm may choose any one of them.)</p>\n<p>This formulation presents problems when I need to prove some property about the output of the algorithm. If I have a property <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span></span></span></span> and a theorem, \"Property <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span></span></span></span> holds for a remainder <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span> outputted by the division algorithm,\" the only way to formalize this, as far as I can see, is to write:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MvPolynomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MvPolynomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">),</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Finsupp</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">linearCombination</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">MvPolynomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">σ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toSyn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toSyn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">support</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">¬</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">degree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>In other words, to repeat the conclusion of <code>MonomialOrder.div</code> (and hence the proof), and prove that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> holds in the course of that proof. This leads to a lot of code duplication.</p>\n<p>(The alternative would be to to extract <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span> from <code>MonomialOrder.div</code> and then try to show that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> holds from the properties we are given about <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span>. But, because the remainder <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span> is not unique, I don't think this proof strategy will always work. For example, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P (r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> may express some invariant that is preserved by every step of the recursive construction, but which does not follow directly from the three postconditions in <code>MonomialOrder.div</code>).</p>\n<p>The alternative would be to write a separate function encoding the division algorithm and producing a pair <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(g, r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>. Then, one could prove properties about the output of this function, such as the three properties in the statement of `MonomialOrder.div'.  (This seems to be how <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Polynomial/Div.html#Polynomial.divByMonic\">single-variable polynomial division</a> is done).</p>\n<p>Without other input (e.g., some selection strategy), this function would be noncomputable, since at various moments the algorithm has a choice of which step to take next and <code>Classical.choice</code> could noncomputably make that choice. This is the main disadvantage that I see.</p>\n<p>Would this be separation be a reasonable refactor? Or are there strong reasons for preferring the current version?</p>",
        "id": 611406470,
        "sender_full_name": "Robert Shlyakhtenko",
        "timestamp": 1784348796
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MonomialOrder.div#doc\">docs#MonomialOrder.div</a> accepts a (not-necessarily-finite) indexed-family of polynomials. Perhaps we should have a version that takes a list of polynomials, and maybe one with <code>[LinearOrder ι]</code> which is the order by which to divide? (or just <code>ι := ℕ</code>)</p>",
        "id": 611408211,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1784350314
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>cc <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"130609\">@Antoine Chambert-Loir</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"331356\">@Hagb (Junyu Guo)</span></p>",
        "id": 611408213,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1784350316
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>The alternative would be to to extract <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span> from <code>MonomialOrder.div</code> and then try to show that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> holds from the properties we are given about <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>We proposed something similar, <code>MonomialOrder.IsRemainder</code> in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/34873\">#34873</a>. (It is a bit out-of-date. We will update it in the near few weeks.)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P (r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> may express some invariant that is preserved by every step of the recursive construction, but which does not follow directly from the three postconditions in <code>MonomialOrder.div</code>. ... The alternative would be to write a separate function encoding the division algorithm and producing a pair <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(g, r)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">g</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think maybe it is good to consider the formalization <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"287929\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Confluence.20API.20for.20polynomial.20reduction/with/603664845\">#mathlib4 &gt; Confluence API for polynomial reduction</a>  (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40368\">#40368</a>) of the reduction and normal form by  <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"932857\">@Park Sanghyeok</span>, which is more general than a function encoding the division algorithm, and might properly cover most use cases of such a function unless the selection strategy of the divisors (<code>g</code> in <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MonomialOrder.div#doc\">docs#MonomialOrder.div</a> or <code>B</code> in <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MonomialOrder.div_set#doc\">docs#MonomialOrder.div_set</a>) matters?</p>",
        "id": 611411445,
        "sender_full_name": "Hagb (Junyu Guo)",
        "timestamp": 1784352907
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"331356\">Hagb (Junyu Guo)</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Division.20algorithm.20for.20multivariate.20polynomials/near/611411445\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I think maybe it is good to consider the formalization <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"287929\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Confluence.20API.20for.20polynomial.20reduction/with/611426080\">#mathlib4 &gt; Confluence API for polynomial reduction</a>  (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40368\">#40368</a>) of the reduction and normal form by  <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"932857\">Park Sanghyeok</span>, which is more general than a function encoding the division algorithm, and might properly cover most use cases of such a function unless the selection strategy of the divisors (<code>g</code> in <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MonomialOrder.div#doc\">docs#MonomialOrder.div</a> or <code>B</code> in <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MonomialOrder.div_set#doc\">docs#MonomialOrder.div_set</a>) matters?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Thanks -- I will take a look!</p>",
